course: everyday
lesson: 277
title: Covering up, false alarms and playing fair
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: People sometimes lie for reasons that feel good at the moment: to protect a friend, to hide embarrassment, or to get out of something. Usually there is an honest way that works better.
explain: Being loyal to a friend means being kind to them, not lying to cover harm they did. Saying «I don't understand» is honest and brave, and «Thanks, but I can't come» is a polite no that needs no made-up excuse.
explain: Some lies are dangerous: pretending to be hurt, lying to a doctor, or making a fake emergency call. In the old story «The Boy Who Cried Wolf», a boy shouts «Wolf!» for fun so often that, when a real wolf comes, nobody believes him.
explain: Games work only when players are honest: the banker handles the money fairly, a tagged player becomes «it», and a footballer does not fake a fall.
explain: Trust is earned by what people do over time, not by how often they say «trust me».
fact: Loyalty does not mean lying for a friend; covering up can let harm continue and drags you into the trouble too.
fact: Saying «I don't understand» is honest and brave, and teachers want to know so they can help.
fact: Pretending to understand means you miss the help you need.
fact: There are honest, polite ways to say no to an invitation, like «Thanks, but I can't come», so you rarely need to invent an excuse.
fact: False alarms, like pretending to be hurt, make it harder for people to be believed later.
fact: Tell doctors and nurses the truth about how you got hurt; they need true facts to treat you, and they are there to help, not to punish.
fact: Never prank call an emergency number (911, 999, 112, 000 or 111); it ties up help someone really needs and is against the law.
fact: Judge people by what they do over time, not by how often they say «trust me».
fact: In a game, being trusted with the bank is a test of honesty, and games rely on players being honest about what happened.
fact: Faking a fall in football, called diving, is cheating and can earn a yellow card.
exercise: A friend asks you to tell the teacher he was with you when he actually broke a window. What does real loyalty mean here? | Staying kind to him without lying to cover it up | Saying whatever he asks, because friends stick together | Blaming a different child instead | Refusing ever to speak to him again
exercise: Why can covering up for a friend's harmful act backfire? | The harm goes on and you are dragged into the trouble too | Teachers never believe two children at once | Your friend will get a bigger prize | It only backfires if the window was expensive and the school makes someone pay for it
exercise: A girl in Ohio did not understand the fractions lesson but nodded when asked. What would have been better? | Saying «I don't understand» so the teacher could help | Copying a friend's work later at home, so the teacher never knows | Nodding again so nobody notices | Skipping the next math lesson
exercise: What does a child lose by pretending to understand a lesson? | The help they need to really learn it | Nothing, because they will learn it later anyway | Only the chance to sit at the front | Their place in the school choir
exercise: A boy does not want to go to a party. What is an honest polite answer? | «Thanks for inviting me, but I can't come» | «I'm sick», even though he is not ill at all | «My dog ate the invitation» | Saying nothing and not turning up
exercise: A girl often says she is hurt to get attention when she is fine. What can happen? | People may not believe her when she really is hurt | She will get more friends each time | Teachers will give her more breaks | Nothing, because nobody keeps count of how often she says it
exercise: What is the lesson of «The Boy Who Cried Wolf»? | False alarms make people stop believing you | Wolves only come to noisy villages | You should never look after sheep on your own | Shouting loudly always brings help, however often you do it
exercise: A boy hurt his arm climbing a fence he was told to stay off. The nurse asks how. What should he do? | Tell the truth so the nurse can treat him properly | Say he fell over in the playground at break | Say he cannot remember | Refuse to answer until his mum arrives
exercise: Why should you tell doctors exactly how an injury happened? | They need true facts to treat you properly | They will tell your teacher everything | They only treat children who were careful | It makes the visit shorter for your parents
exercise: A child phones 000 in Australia as a joke. Why is this wrong? | It ties up help someone in a real emergency needs | It only costs a little money | The operator might laugh too much | It is only wrong at night, when the operators are trying to rest
exercise: Someone keeps saying «trust me, trust me» but often breaks promises. How should you judge them? | By what they actually do over time | By how many times they say «trust me» | By how loudly they say it | By how friendly their smile is
exercise: You are the banker in Monopoly and nobody is looking at the money. What is right? | Handle the bank honestly | Slip yourself a little extra, it is only a game | Give extra money to your best friend | Hide some notes for later
exercise: In tag, a girl is clearly touched but shouts «You missed!». What should she have done? | Admit it and become «it» | Run faster so it does not count | Say she was on «base» even if she wasn't | Stop playing so nobody wins
exercise: A footballer falls over on purpose to win a free kick. What is this called and is it fair? | Diving, and it is cheating | Tackling, and it is allowed | A dummy, and it is clever | Offside, and it is a rule
test: Your best friend scribbled on a library book and asks you to say you didn't see anything. Which is TRUE? | You can stay her friend without lying to cover the damage | Real friends always lie for each other | It is only a lie if the librarian asks you twice about the same book | Saying nothing is the same as telling
test: In science you are lost but everyone else seems to get it. What is the brave choice? | Put your hand up and say you don't understand | Pretend and hope it comes up in the test | Look busy so the teacher moves on to the next part of the lesson | Wait and ask nobody
test: A classmate invites you to go swimming and you do not want to. Which answer is honest and kind? | «Thanks, but I can't come this time» | «I've got a broken leg, so I can't swim» | «My parents say that you are rude to me» | «I'm going to the moon that day, sorry»
test: A boy shouted «I'm stuck!» from a tree three times as a joke. The fourth time he really is stuck. Why might nobody hurry? | His false alarms taught them not to believe him | Trees are always safe to climb | People never help in the afternoon | He shouted in the wrong voice, so nobody could tell it was really him
test: What is the main reason it is against the law to prank call 911 or 999? | It can delay help for someone in a real emergency | It makes the operators tired of their job | Phones may break if used that way | It costs the caller a large fee each time they ring the number
test: A new kid says «You can totally trust me» every few minutes but took your pen and didn't give it back. What is wise? | Judge him by what he does, not what he says | Trust him fully because he said so many times | Never speak to anyone new again | Ask him to say it louder
test: A girl twists her ankle doing a forbidden trick on the trampoline and is scared of being told off. What should she tell the doctor? | The truth, because the doctor needs it to treat her | A story about tripping on the stairs at home | Nothing, doctors can guess | Whatever her friend tells her to say, so nobody gets in trouble
test: During a board game the banker «forgets» to pay you but always pays himself. What is going wrong? | He is not being honest with the money trusted to him | He is just better at the game | Bankers are allowed to keep extra | It is fair because he is the banker
test: In a netball match a girl pretends she was pushed so her team gets a penalty. Which is TRUE? | Faking a foul is cheating, even if the referee is fooled | It is smart play and just part of the game at every level | It is fine if the score is close | It is only cheating in the final
test: mate broke the window n wants me to say he was with me. do i?? | No, you can stay his friend without lying for him | Yes, real mates always cover for each other, whatever happens | Yes, but only if nobody else actually saw him do it | No, and never ever talk to him again after this day
test: didnt get the fractions at all but i nodded lol. bad? | Yes, tell the teacher you don't get it | No, you'll pick it up later by yourself, everyone does | No, just copy a friend's answers later | Yes, so skip the next maths lesson
test: my bro thinks prank calling 999 is funny, is it tho | No, it blocks help for real emergencies and it's illegal | Yes, the operators enjoy a laugh too | Only if he hangs up really fast | Only if he calls from a friend's phone
test: kid at footy fell over on purpose to get a free kick, allowed? | No, that's diving and it's cheating | Yes, it's a clever part of the game | Yes, if the ref doesn't notice | Only when the score is level
explain: A boy in Leeds told the teacher his friend had been with him when the friend had really been bullying a younger child; the bullying went on for weeks and both boys ended up in serious trouble.
explain: A girl in Arizona kept pretending to faint for attention; when she really did fall and hurt her wrist, other children thought it was a joke and nobody fetched help for a while.
fact: Every prank call to an emergency number takes a call taker away from someone who may be in real danger.
fact: People who cry wolf too often are not believed when they really need help.
test: mate wants me to say he was with me yesterday, he wasnt | Say no kindly, lying can let harm go on | Say yes, that's what friends do | Say yes but cross your fingers behind your back | Say yes if he gives you sweets
test: lets prank call 911 for a laugh | No, it can stop help reaching someone in danger | It's fine if you hang up fast before they answer | It's fine if you call from a friend's phone instead | It's fine if nobody gets hurt and it's only a joke
test: didnt get the lesson but everyone else did, pretend? | Say you don't understand, the teacher will help | Pretend and hope it makes sense later on by itself | Copy a friend's work instead and say nothing at all | Stay home the next day so you miss the next lesson
test: did u hear Jake's mate lied to cover for him when he broke the window and now "both are suspended for like a month". lesson? | Covering up for a friend drags you into the trouble too | Good friends always lie for each other | Lying is fine if you don't get caught | Only cover up for your best friend
test: someone said a kid prank called 911 and "the police came with 10 cars and arrested his whole family". what's prob exaggerated | The 10 cars and the arrests; prank calls are still illegal | That prank calls tie up help | That 911 is an emergency number | That prank calls are wrong
test: everyone says the girl who faked an injury at netball is "a total liar, never believe her again". pass it on? | No, don't spread it; false alarms hurt trust | Yes, everyone should be warned about her before the next match | Yes, and add more stories about her | Yes, but only to the team
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises the relationship between loyalty and the concealment of a friend's wrongdoing? | Loyalty does not require lying, and concealment may let harm continue | Loyalty obliges one to conceal any wrongdoing of a friend | Concealment is acceptable provided no adult enquires | Concealment protects the friend from all consequences
exam: Identify the principal reason for which hoax calls to an emergency number are prohibited. | They divert call takers from persons in genuine danger | They incur a small charge to the caller's family | They are merely considered impolite | They cause the telephone line to fail for the whole of the district
exam: It is given that a pupil does not comprehend a lesson. Select the response consistent with honesty and with the pupil's interest. | The pupil shall state that they do not understand | The pupil shall pretend to understand to avoid embarrassment | The pupil shall copy the work of a classmate | The pupil shall remain silent and await the next lesson
exam: State which of the following describes the act known in football as diving. | Feigning a fall to deceive the referee, which is cheating | A permitted technique for shielding the ball | A legitimate method of winning possession | A tackle made from behind an opponent
